{"nodes": [{"id": "src_chrono_thirdparty_yaml_cpp_test_googletest_1_13_0_docs_faq_googletest_faq", "label": "GoogleTest FAQ", "file_type": "document", "source_file": "src/chrono_thirdparty/yaml-cpp/test/googletest-1.13.0/docs/faq.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "src_chrono_thirdparty_yaml_cpp_test_googletest_1_13_0_docs_faq_underscore_naming_rule", "label": "No underscores in test suite or test names", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "src/chrono_thirdparty/yaml-cpp/test/googletest-1.13.0/docs/faq.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "TEST(Suite, Name) generates the class Suite_Name_Test, so a leading or trailing underscore produces an identifier reserved to the implementation (leading _ plus uppercase, or a double underscore). Interior underscores are legal but let two different TESTs collide on one generated class name, e.g. TEST(Time, Flies_Like_An_Arrow) and TEST(Time_Flies, Like_An_Arrow). The blanket ban is stricter than strictly necessary, chosen for simplicity and to leave room for implementation changes."}, {"id": "src_chrono_thirdparty_yaml_cpp_test_googletest_1_13_0_docs_faq_disabled_prefix", "label": "DISABLED_ prefix", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "src/chrono_thirdparty/yaml-cpp/test/googletest-1.13.0/docs/faq.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "One of the special keywords for which GoogleTest reserves the underscore character, which is part of why user names may not contain one."}, {"id": "src_chrono_thirdparty_yaml_cpp_test_googletest_1_13_0_docs_faq_ctor_vs_setup", "label": "Constructor/destructor vs SetUp()/TearDown()", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "src/chrono_thirdparty/yaml-cpp/test/googletest-1.13.0/docs/faq.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "A fresh fixture object is built for every TEST_F. Constructors are usually preferred because members can be const and base-class construction/destruction order is guaranteed, whereas a subclass can forget to chain SetUp/TearDown. SetUp/TearDown are required when the logic must dispatch virtually, when it needs ASSERT_* (unusable in a ctor/dtor), or when tear-down can throw, since throwing in a destructor is undefined behavior."}, {"id": "src_chrono_thirdparty_yaml_cpp_test_googletest_1_13_0_docs_faq_death_test_suite_ordering", "label": "Why the whole suite must be named *DeathTest", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "src/chrono_thirdparty/yaml-cpp/test/googletest-1.13.0/docs/faq.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "GoogleTest never interleaves suites, because each suite is set up once before its first test and torn down after its last. Ordering by individual test name instead of suite name would create unsatisfiable constraints when two suites each mix death and non-death tests."}, {"id": "src_chrono_thirdparty_yaml_cpp_test_googletest_1_13_0_docs_faq_expect_eq_null", "label": "EXPECT_EQ(NULL, ptr) supported but not EXPECT_NE(NULL, ptr)", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "src/chrono_thirdparty/yaml-cpp/test/googletest-1.13.0/docs/faq.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "src_chrono_thirdparty_yaml_cpp_test_googletest_1_13_0_docs_faq_typed_vs_value_param", "label": "Typed tests vs value-parameterized tests for interface conformance", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "src/chrono_thirdparty/yaml-cpp/test/googletest-1.13.0/docs/faq.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Both can verify that several implementations satisfy shared requirements; the FAQ weighs choosing between repeating over a type list and repeating over factory-function values."}, {"id": "src_chrono_thirdparty_yaml_cpp_test_googletest_1_13_0_docs_faq_fixtures_over_globals", "label": "Prefer test fixtures over global variables", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "src/chrono_thirdparty/yaml-cpp/test/googletest-1.13.0/docs/faq.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "A fresh fixture per test keeps state from leaking between tests, which globals cannot guarantee."}, {"id": "src_chrono_thirdparty_yaml_cpp_test_googletest_1_13_0_docs_faq_death_test_log_suppression", "label": "Death-test child LOG messages printed only on failure", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "src/chrono_thirdparty/yaml-cpp/test/googletest-1.13.0/docs/faq.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Forwarding the child's logs on success would make real problems harder to find in the parent's log; the only workaround is deliberately breaking the death test, which the docs concede is a hack pending fork-and-exec death tests."}, {"id": "src_chrono_thirdparty_yaml_cpp_test_googletest_1_13_0_docs_faq_death_test_state_loss", "label": "Death test state changes are lost", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "src/chrono_thirdparty/yaml-cpp/test/googletest-1.13.0/docs/faq.md", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "The statement runs in a forked child, so mutations never reach the parent process running the rest of the suite."}], "edges": [{"source": "src_chrono_thirdparty_yaml_cpp_test_googletest_1_13_0_docs_faq_googletest_faq", "target": "src_chrono_thirdparty_yaml_cpp_test_googletest_1_13_0_docs_faq_underscore_naming_rule", "relation": "references", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1.0, "source_file": "src/chrono_th